        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Grand Canal of China</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 486 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Built in stages over many centuries: the Han Canal for King Fuchai of Wu from 486 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> network under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a> by conscripted labour, then summit sections under the Yuan and a renovation under the Ming</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A system of canals, canalised rivers and lakes; the main Jing–Hang stem is thought to extend for 1,776 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, in six main subsections, with a single summit section and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-lock">pound locks</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> and the municipalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river">Qiantang River</a> to Tongzhou on the edge of Beijing, with the Eastern Zhejiang Canal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the longest artificial waterway in the world; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> network of 604 to 609; the grain shipments to the north; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-lock">pound lock</a>; the summit sections of the Yuan; its use as the eastern route of the South-North Water Diversion Project; its World Heritage listing in 2014</span></div>
        </div>
