    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The oldest sections were dug in the early 5th century BCE for the states of Wu and Yue, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a> joined and extended them between 604 and 609 to link Jiangnan with his capital and his armies, using massive levies of conscripted labour.</li><li>For centuries the canal carried the grain collected in taxes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> Delta to the north, and each dynasty treated its repair and the control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>'s floods as a duty.</li><li>Floods, wars and the shifting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> repeatedly broke the canal, and after 1855 its northern and southern parts stayed separate for decades; it was rebuilt after 1949, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2014.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>By the year 735 about 150 million kilograms of grain were recorded as shipped each year along the canal, and in the mid-15th century 121,500 soldiers and officers were needed simply to operate its 11,775 government grain barges.</p></aside>
    </section>

