    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">486 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">King Fuchai of Wu began the Han Canal, which within three years connected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">609</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a> completed the network after levies of conscripted labour, begun in 604, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> and the northeast</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">984</span><span class="rf-text">Qiao Weiyue, an Assistant Commissioner of Transport for Huainan, invented the double-gate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-lock">pound lock</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1128</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>'s governor Du Chong broke the dykes holding back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, and the floods ruined much of the canal</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1280s</span><span class="rf-text">A summit section was dug across the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> massif, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> by a direct waterway for the first time</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1411</span><span class="rf-text">The Ming renovated the canal almost entirely in the years to 1415, using 165,000 labourers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1855</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> flooded and changed its course, severing the canal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2014</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage Conference listed the Grand Canal as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yu-the-great.jpg" alt="Yu the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A7%E7%A6%B9%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4%E5%9C%96.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Grand Canal is a system of interconnected canals linking major rivers and lakes in North and East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It served as an important waterborne transport route between the north and the south in medieval and premodern China. Its oldest sections were completed in the early 5th century BCE, during the conflicts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>, to provide supplies and transport routes for the states of Wu and Yue. King Fuchai of Wu, whose capital was in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, ventured north to attack the State of Qi and ordered a canal built for trade and to ship supplies north in case his forces should engage the northern states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Lu. This became the Han Canal. Work began in 486 BCE south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, and within three years it had connected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>, using existing waterways, lakes and marshes.</p><p>The Han Canal is known as the second oldest section, since the Hong Canal most likely preceded it. The Hong Canal linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> to the Si and Bian rivers and became the model for the shape of the canal in the north. Its date is uncertain. The diplomat Su <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> first mentioned it in 330 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> knew of no historical date for it and discussed it just after the legendary works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>, and modern scholars now consider it to belong to the 6th century BCE. The Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> Canal began as the Shanyin Canal, excavated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Shaoxing</a> by the Yue official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-li">Fan Li</a> in the early 5th century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The main stem, the Jing–Hang Grand Canal, is thought to extend for 1,776 km, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in the north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> in the south, and is divided into six main subsections. The Jiangnan Canal runs from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river">Qiantang River</a> at Hangzhou around the east side of Lake Tai, through Jiaxing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> and Wuxi, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> at Zhenjiang. The Inner Canal runs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> at Huai'an, and the Middle Canal from Huai'an to Luoma Lake and then to the Nansi Lakes. The Lu Canal continues through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to the Wei at Linqing, the Southern Canal runs from Linqing to the Hai River at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>, and the Northern Canal runs from Tianjin to Tongzhou on the outskirts of Beijing. The route passes through the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, Shandong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> and the municipalities of Tianjin and Beijing. In 2014 the Eastern Zhejiang Canal from Hangzhou to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a> was also recognised as an official component.</p><p>Though the canal nominally crosses the watersheds of five river systems, the variation between them is so low that it has only a single summit section. The water flows from Beijing toward Tianjin, from Nanwang north toward Tianjin and from Nanwang south toward Yangzhou. The canal did not form a single continuous man-made channel but used artificial channels together with canalised and natural waterways. Along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> canals ran an imperial roadway with stables at regular intervals for a rapid courier system, and lines of trees were planted along parts of the canal as windbreaks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui.jpg" alt="Emperor Yang of Sui" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sui_Yangdi_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yan Liben, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> ended three centuries of chaos and allowed the repair of existing canals and the construction of new ones. Their main purpose was to tap the resources of Jiangnan in the southeast to enrich the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and to supply the Sui armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goguryeo-sui-war">Goguryeo–Sui War</a>. A levee project of 587 under the engineer Liang Rui established lock gates to regulate water levels. By 600 silt had built up in the Hong Canal, and the chief engineer Yuwen Kai advised digging a new canal parallel to it. With the recorded labour of five million people under the supervision of Ma Shumou, the first major section, the Bian Qu, was completed in 605. The whole network was completed between 604 and 609 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>, again with massive levies of conscripted labour, as the Record of the Opening of the Canal describes at length.</p><p>Later builders added to it. The Mongol Oqruqči engineered the Jizhou Canal in 1238 as the first true summit section, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitong-canal">Huitong Canal</a> was built in 1289 by the engineer Ma Zhizhen after a geological survey. A summit section dug across the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> massif in the 1280s shortened the overall length by as much as 700 km. Between 1411 and 1415 the Ming dammed the Wen River to feed 60% of its water north into the canal, dug four large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> in Shandong, and used 165,000 labourers to dredge the bed and build new channels, embankments and locks. In 984 Qiao Weiyue invented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-lock">pound lock</a>, which let ships wait in a gated space while the water was drained to the right level.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani.jpg" alt="Rashid al-Din Hamadani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presentation_of_the_book,_folio_6_(Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France,_Suppl%C3%A9ment_Persan_1113).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jami al-Tawarikh artist, 1420-1430 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tang capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> was the most thriving metropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in its day, but the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, close to the canal, was the economic hub of the era, as headquarters of the government salt monopoly and the largest pre-modern industrial production centre of the empire. One of the greatest benefits of the canal in the Tang and later dynasties was that it reduced the cost of shipping tax grain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> Delta to northern China. Officials in charge of the canal and nearby salt works grew enormously wealthy. The canal played a major role in periodically reuniting northern and southern China, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> it made or broke the fortunes of cities along its route: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, better placed on the main artery than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, became Ming China's greatest economic centre.</p><p>The costs fell on those who built and ran it. The unpopular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goguryeo-sui-war">Goguryeo–Sui War</a> and the massive civil conscription involved in creating the canals were among the chief factors in the rebellions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>'s reign and the rapid fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>. The scholar Gu Yanwu estimated that the Ming had to employ 47,004 full-time labourers recruited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lijia-system">lijia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> system to maintain the canal. Travellers admired it, among them the monk Ennin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, the Korean official Choe Bu, who travelled its whole length in 1488, and the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a>. In 1169 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> envoy Lou Yue recorded his journey north along it in a diary.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The canal's history is one of damage and repair. Sections degraded during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/five-dynasties-and-ten-kingdoms-period">Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and periodic flooding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> eroded and threatened it; during wars the rivers' high dikes were sometimes deliberately breached. In 858 an enormous flood along the canal inundated thousands of acres of farmland and killed tens of thousands of people. After 1128, when Du Chong broke the dykes and dams against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> invaders, a series of floods shifted the Yellow River south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, and much of the canal was ruined for several years. Even so, restoration of the canal and its flood control works was assumed as a duty by each successive dynasty. A Chinese official remarked in 1447 that the flood-prone Yellow River made the canal like a throat that could easily be strangled.</p><p>In 1855 the Yellow River flooded and changed course, severing the canal in Shandong, and the dikes were opened to flood the advancing troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a>. The difficulty of crossing the Yellow River, a sea route for grain ships and the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>–Pukou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>–Hankou railways left the canal to languish, and for decades its northern and southern parts stayed separate. Some sections fell into disrepair and some were returned to flat fields. After 1949 the authorities ordered heavy reconstruction, and the portion south of the Yellow River remains in heavy use by barges carrying bulk cargo, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, sand and construction materials.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Grand Canal has been upgraded to serve as the eastern route of the South-North Water Diversion Project, which pumps water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> at Jiangdu near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, repumps it through 23 stations to the watershed at Nanwang and carries it by gravity to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>. Industrialisation left the canal greatly polluted: by the 1990s barge crews could tell when they neared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> by the stench of the black water, and fishermen on Dongping Lake objected when water from the Yangtze appeared to kill fish. In the 21st century efforts to improve conditions have increased. Around Hangzhou a $250 million restoration project begun in 2001 improved water quality until the water no longer produced a noticeable odor and could support some fauna.</p><p>On 22 June 2014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage Conference listed the Grand Canal as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, a complex of 31 sections and a number of accessory heritage sites. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Grand Canal Museum opened in 2021. Within China the canal is credited with enabling cultural exchange and political integration between north and south and with serving as the economic lifeline of indigenous trade, and rulers used it for tours of inspection: the Kangxi and Qianlong emperors made twelve trips to the south, on all occasions but one reaching Hangzhou. At Tongzhou, where the modern canal stops, a Grand Canal Cultural Park has been built.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Grand Canal is a system of linked canals in North and East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, begun with the Han Canal in 486 BCE and completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a> in 609. It carried grain and trade between north and south for centuries, was broken by floods and shifts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, and was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2014.</em></p>

